# Source code in the repository is LF, always, even when the working copy on
# Windows is CRLF.
#
# Without this line the behaviour depends on core.autocrlf, that is, on a
# **machine-global** setting and not on the project: where it is on the diffs
# come out clean, and on a clone where it is not the first change to a file would
# rewrite every one of its lines. A two-hundred-line diff for one changed line
# hides the change, which is the only thing the diff was there to show.
*.go      text eol=lf
*.md      text eol=lf
*.js      text eol=lf
*.css     text eol=lf
*.html    text eol=lf
*.json    text eol=lf
*.mod     text eol=lf
*.sum     text eol=lf

# The baselines are ours and are read, not redistributed, so they follow the
# repository's LF like everything else we write. `licenses/**` below is the
# opposite case and takes it back for the licence texts: **that line has to stay
# after this one**, because the last rule that matches is the one that wins.
*.txt     text eol=lf

# build.ps1 is run by PowerShell on Windows: there CRLF is the convention.
*.ps1     text eol=crlf

# The workflows are read by Linux and Windows runners alike and never by a
# Windows editor, so they follow the repository's LF like the source. Without a
# line of their own they would fall back to core.autocrlf, which is the machine
# setting this whole file exists to stop mattering — and YAML is the format
# where a stray CR is least likely to announce itself.
*.yml     text eol=lf
*.yaml    text eol=lf

# The files that carry the rules follow them: with no extension to match they
# fall back to core.autocrlf, which is the machine setting this whole file
# exists to stop mattering.
.gitattributes  text eol=lf
.gitignore      text eol=lf

# The two artefacts we ship without producing them: libopus compiled to
# WebAssembly, and the recogniser's weights. Git already treats them as binary
# and today it is right -- it looks for a NUL in the first bytes -- so nothing
# is broken. But that is a heuristic reading the content, not a rule about the
# file, and a converted `.wasm` would be a corruption with no error anywhere.
# `binary` says it outright, and stops `git diff` rendering six megabytes of
# weights as text.
*.wasm    binary
*.gguf    binary

# The social preview card. The same argument as the two above, with one
# difference: this one is ours, and the file that makes it is committed beside
# it (images/social-preview.html). Saying it outright also stops a diff rendering
# a hundred kilobytes of PNG as text.
*.png     binary

# And the one picture that is a photograph rather than a page: a webcam frame
# fills a PNG eleven times over, so images/viewer.jpg is a JPEG and needs the
# same line. It is here rather than only beside the file because the next
# photograph will not remember to ask.
*.jpg     binary

# Licence texts are reproduced **as they are**. They have no extension, and
# without a line of their own core.autocrlf converts them, that is, a setting of
# the machine: the working copy would hold bytes different from those written by
# whoever wrote the licence. For code that is irrelevant, for a text that is
# redistributed verbatim it is not.
LICENSE     -text
NOTICE      -text
licenses/** -text
